      "content": "[]{#obs label=\"obs\"} In the setting of random partitions, the algebra of polynomial functions[^1] on Young diagrams and its various bases play an extremely important role that can be compared to the one played by the moments of a real random variable. Hence, the **generating function** of a Young diagram $\\lambda$ is defined by $$G_{\\lambda}(z)=\\frac{\\prod_{i=1}^{v-1}z-y_{i}}{\\prod_{i=1}^{v}z-x_{i}},$$ where $x_{1}<y_{1}<x_{2}<y_{2}<\\cdots<x_{v-1}<y_{v-1}<x_{v}$ are the **interlacing coordinates** of $\\lambda$, that is to say, the sequences of local minima and local maxima of the function $s\\m",
